/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Converter P drives current +Id into the load; converter N, connected the other way round, drives −Id.
   - α1 and α2 are the firing angles of P and N in degrees.
   - The four quadrants are then: P rectifying (+V, +I, forward motoring); N inverting (+V, −I, forward braking, power returned); N rectifying (−V, −I, reverse motoring); P inverting (−V, +I, reverse braking).
   - Equal average voltages are not equal instantaneous voltages: each bridge's output has a different ripple shape.
   - In both modes the firing logic must never let both bridges conduct the load current with no reactor in the loop, which would short-circuit the supply through two thyristors.
   - A reversing rolling mill drives the metal one way, brakes, and drives it back, many times a minute.
*/
