/* book Worked example 2: Q: A three-phase dual converter on 415 V, 50 Hz supplies a DC motor with armature resistance 0.5 Ω. (a) Motoring at 50 A with back EMF E = 400 V: find α of converter P. (b) At the same speed (E still 400 V) the motor is braked at 50 A through converter N: find the terminal voltage, αN, and the power returned.
   1. Vd0 = 3√2 × 415/π = 560.45 V.
   2. (a) Vt = E + Id R = 400 + 50 × 0.5 = 425 V. cos α1 = 425/560.45 = 0.7583, α1 = 40.7°.
   3. (b) The current is now −50 A, so Vt = E + Id R = 400 − 25 = 375 V at the motor.
   4. Converter N sees this voltage with reversed polarity: its own output must be −375 V, so cos αN = −375/560.45 = −0.6691 and αN = 132.0°. N is inverting.
   5. Power returned to the supply = 375 × 50 = 18.75 kW. Check: the machine gives 400 × 50 = 20.0 kW and the armature loses 50² × 0.5 = 1.25 kW.
   Answer: (a) α1 = 40.7°; (b) Vt = 375 V, αN = 132.0°, 18.75 kW returned. */
