/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The capacitor charges to the mains peak, and the diodes conduct only while the mains voltage is above the capacitor voltage: in the filter example of this unit, for about 19° before each peak.
   - A capacitor never corrects this; it causes it.
   - The diode bridge (with only a small filter capacitor) gives a rectified sine |vs|.
   - The control has two loops.
   - Re is the emulated input resistance in Ω, Vs the RMS mains voltage in V and P the input power in W; D is the switch duty ratio; ΔiL the peak-to-peak inductor ripple in A, L in H and fs the switching frequency in Hz.
   - A boost converter can only raise voltage, so Vo must stay above the mains peak (325 V for 230 V mains); near each zero crossing D approaches 1.
   - Interleaved PFC runs two boost cells 180° apart, so their ripple currents partly cancel and each inductor is smaller.
   - The PFC stage shapes the current and regulates the bus; a second DC-DC stage normally provides isolation and the low output voltage.
*/
